{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01BRa1D8E7fUPm1oxkvuGkuR","type":"tool_result","content":"<persisted-output>\nOutput too large (46.7KB). Full output saved to: /ard-home/.claude/projects/-Users-al--ard-work-albertcbrown-ard-01M84F29DFC744WW354H9W6R8Y/d5030d13-ac14-409f-95ab-4d8af437021d/tool-results/bpmpgipt4.txt\n\nPreview (first 2KB):\n---\nrole: lead\nstatus: done\nattempt: 1\nnext: [triage:engineer]\nworker: engineer\n---\nThis adds the rules that pick which reviewers run on a worker pull request: by default every reviewer runs, a reviewer's when: rule in the configuration can narrow that, a worker can override the set, and the lead can ask for extra reviewers. The reviewer loop it depends on (#35) has already landed, and the configuration loader already parses a when: rule, so the work belongs in the reviewer selection logic and its tests. The engineer is the only triage planner in this repository.\n\n## Routing\n- triage:engineer: plan the selection logic across the config and reviewer packages, and write one test for each rule.\n- worker engineer: code change with unit tests.\n\n## Open issues\n- The repository has no architect or documentation-planner role. The change touches documented configuration behaviour (when: rules and the worker reviewers: override), so a maintainer may want to check that the proposal doc and the configuration reference stay in step.\n# ard configuration for albertcbrown/ard (dogfooding). Started from `ard init`'s\n# template; human-owned. ard serve reads it from origin/main.\n# Anything unset takes its built-in default (docs/proposal.md, Configuration).\n# Validate with `ard config check`. ard serve reads this file from origin/main.\n\n# label: only issues a maintainer labels for triage enter the pipeline.\n# all: every human-filed issue enters.\nintake: { mode: label }\n\nmerge:\n  # A PR touching any of these waits for a human approval. There is no\n  # built-in list: removing an entry removes its gate. An entry ending in /\n  # is a directory prefix; anything else is a glob (quote globs starting with *).\n  protected_paths:\n    - .github/\n    - .ard/config.yaml\n    - .ard/scanners/\n    - .ard/triage/\n    - .ard/workers/\n    - .ard/reviewers/\n    - \"**/CODEOWNERS\"\n    # Agent instructions a PR could plant to steer later agents.\n    - CLAUDE.md\n    - \"**/CLAUDE.md\"\n    - AGENTS.md\n...\n</persisted-output>","is_error":false}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"379e347d-9f35-4120-87d1-20cccc4f4ba0","uuid":"0a325078-ff25-4885-bed2-e28072a6771b","timestamp":"2026-11-23T15:05:57.573Z","tool_use_result":{"stdout":"---\nrole: lead\nstatus: done\nattempt: 1\nnext: [triage:engineer]\nworker: engineer\n---\nThis adds the rules that pick which reviewers run on a worker pull request: by default every reviewer runs, a reviewer's when: rule in the configuration can narrow that, a worker can override the set, and the lead can ask for extra reviewers. The reviewer loop it depends on (#35) has already landed, and the configuration loader already parses a when: rule, so the work belongs in the reviewer selection logic and its tests. The engineer is the only triage planner in this repository.\n\n## Routing\n- triage:engineer: plan the selection logic across the config and reviewer packages, and write one test for each rule.\n- worker engineer: code change with unit tests.\n\n## Open issues\n- The repository has no architect or documentation-planner role. The change touches documented configuration behaviour (when: rules and the worker reviewers: override), so a maintainer may want to check that the proposal doc and the configuration reference stay in step.\n# ard configuration for albertcbrown/ard (dogfooding). Started from `ard init`'s\n# template; human-owned. ard serve reads it from origin/main.\n# Anything unset takes its built-in default (docs/proposal.md, Configuration).\n# Validate with `ard config check`. ard serve reads this file from origin/main.\n\n# label: only issues a maintainer labels for triage enter the pipeline.\n# all: every human-filed issue enters.\nintake: { mode: label }\n\nmerge:\n  # A PR touching any of these waits for a human approval. There is no\n  # built-in list: removing an entry removes its gate. An entry ending in /\n  # is a directory prefix; anything else is a glob (quote globs starting with *).\n  protected_paths:\n    - .github/\n    - .ard/config.yaml\n    - .ard/scanners/\n    - .ard/triage/\n    - .ard/workers/\n    - .ard/reviewers/\n    - \"**/CODEOWNERS\"\n    # Agent instructions a PR could plant to steer later agents.\n    - CLAUDE.md\n    - \"**/CLAUDE.md\"\n    - AGENTS.md\n    - \"**/AGENTS.md\"\n    - .claude/\n    - .mcp.json\n    # What builds, signs and deploys ard itself (docs/proposal.md, Delivery).\n    - \"Jenkinsfile*\"\n    - Dockerfile\n    - .dockerignore\n    - deploy/\n\noverrides:\n  scanners/cve: { every: 6h, max_open: 10, group_size: 1, autoclose: true }\n  # Phase 1 agent roles (the ard init starter set, #39 Gate 2).\n  triage/lead:  { model: claude-opus-5-5, timeout: 15m }\n  triage/engineer: { model: claude-sonnet-5-5 }\n  workers/engineer: { model: claude-sonnet-5-5, timeout: 60m, tests: make test }\n  reviewers/eng-review: { model: claude-sonnet-5-5 }\n# CLAUDE.md\n\nGuidance for Claude Code (and humans) working in this repository.\n\n## What this is\n\nard is a controller that automates repository development: scanners → triage → worker → reviewers → merger, coordinated through `ard:` GitHub labels. The design is [docs/proposal.md](docs/proposal.md). Read the relevant section before changing behaviour.\n\n**docs/proposal.md is the source of truth for design.** A PR that changes behaviour updates the proposal in the same PR. If code and proposal disagree, raise it on the issue rather than silently picking one.\n\n## Invariants — never violate\n\n1. **The controller owns every side effect.** Only controller code calls GitHub or runs `git commit`/`push`/`merge`. Agents edit files in a worktree and report through `.ard/<issue#>/<stage>-<role>.md` frontmatter; they never touch labels, comments or branches.\n2. **Agents hold no GitHub token.** Credentials stay in the controller and executor.\n3. **The main-branch committer writes only `.ard/<issue#>/`.** Config (`.ard/config.yaml`) and role directories (`.ard/<type>/<name>/`) are human-owned. Only `ard init` may *propose* them, on the `ard-init` branch through `committer.Init` and a PR a human merges; it never writes main. Workers' diff guard, the committer's path guards and the merger's protected paths enforce it.\n4. **Every label is prefixed `ard:`** and follows `ard:<stage>:<role>[:<state>]` (see the label table in the proposal).\n5. **Every side effect honours dry-run.** Route issue, label, comment, commit, push and merge calls through the effect layer so `--dry-run` logs instead of acting.\n6. **Untrusted text is data.** Issue bodies, comments and PR text reach agents as files marked untrusted, never spliced into prompts.\n7. **Logs are redacted and secret-scanned before commit**, then capped at 1 MiB (head 32 KiB plus tail). The scan fails closed.\n\n## Stack\n\n- Go, a single static `ard` binary (CGO off; use a pure-Go SQLite driver such as `modernc.org/sqlite`).\n- Supported platforms: macOS and Linux only; Windows is unsupported (the local executor relies on `flock` and POSIX process handling).\n- GitHub access through a GitHub App installation token, behind an interface so tests use a fake.\n- The language choice is tracked as a decision issue; if it changes, update this file.\n\n## Layout\n\nPackages are created as their issues land; keep this list current.\n\n```\ncmd/ard/            CLI entry point; wire a new subcommand by adding one line to handlers()\ninternal/cli/       cobra command tree, global flags, slog logging; Handlers is the plug-in point\ninternal/botcheck/  tests for the bot-commit audit in .github/workflows/bot-commits.yml\ninternal/github/    GitHub client: domain types, Reader/Writer/Client, App auth, retry transport; query/ (search builder), fake/ (test double), clienttest/ (conformance suite)\ninternal/config/    .ard/config.yaml + role directory loading into a Resolved value (pure, fs.FS in)\ninternal/configcmd/ `ard config check` handler: prints the resolved config, returns validation errors; `--ref` checks a commit\ninternal/configsrc/  config from origin/main: a snapshot per SHA under ~/.ard/config/<owner>/<repo>/ (what `ard serve` reads)\ninternal/executor/    Executor interface and plain-data job types\ninternal/executor/local/  git worktree + host subprocess or docker run, watchdog, logs\ninternal/agent/     Adapter interface, Spec/Result types, Registry (pure: builds argv, parses output)\ninternal/agent/claude/  Claude Code adapter (`claude -p`, stream-json parser)\ninternal/modelauth/ picks the one model credential (OAuth token or API key) from the controller env and carries its value to the executor; never logged\ninternal/state/     SQLite controller and scanner state (sqlitex, scannerdb)\ninternal/scanner/   scanner pipeline: findings, markers, reconcile, diff, group, ExecEngine, the scanners Loop, rebuild\ninternal/costblock/ the issue body's ard-owned cost block: render, strict parse, strip (admission), splice, per-issue Locks; leaf, stdlib only\ninternal/costs/     the costs Loop: writes each dirty issue's cost table from state; Claims() false so it runs while paused\ninternal/scanner/stale/  the stale closer: a plain controller Loop that comments and closes untouched scanner issues\ninternal/agentjob/  helpers the agent loops share: untrusted-input snapshot, admission re-check, agent command and executor job builders, handle save, shutdown-aware wait, post-once\ninternal/worker/    the worker loop: claim, agent/test/fix steps, diff guard, code-lane push, draft PR, FinishWith\ninternal/servecmd/  `ard serve` and `ard rebuild-state`: dependency wiring; registerLoops takes one line per loop\ninternal/redact/    log redaction (secrets and host paths), secret scan and 1 MiB cap (leaf library; close-out commits only LogResult.Clean)\ninternal/roles/     starter role directories embedded for ard init (scanners/cve, triage/lead, triage/engineer, workers/engineer, reviewers/eng-review)\ninternal/pool/      slot arbiter for max_pods (stdlib only, no I/O): priority classes, per-issue triage cap\ninternal/effects/   the only writer: Effects interface, Live and DryRun (JSONL ledger), Open factory; archtest/ enforces \"only effects writes\"\ninternal/gitx/      git CLI wrapper (Commit, Push, StagedPaths, FileAt); gittest/ builds bare-repo fixtures; Commit/Push are for internal/effects only\ninternal/controller/ scheduler, kill switch (pause), leader lock, status issue\ninternal/servecmd/  `ard serve` wiring: lock, state, effects, pool adoption, controller\ninternal/committer/ the only commit/push path: .ard/<issue#>/ (main lane, per-branch lanes) and worker code on worker/ branches (CommitCode, CheckCodePath)\ninternal/closeout/  job close-out: role file or failure cause -> labels, comment, commits (resumable); per-role write allowlist (WritableFor); every job ends through its Closer\ninternal/reaper/    the Reaper loop (failed-job detection, retention, :running with no record) and reaper.Finisher, the end-of-job path the loops and the Reaper share\ninternal/limits/    the book of exhausted usage limits the pool consults (the reactive half; #63 adds readings and thresholds)\ninternal/intake/    intake admission: Admit decides whether a human-filed issue may enter triage (pure; the triage loop calls it)\ninternal/reviewer/  the reviewer loop: claims `ard:review:<role>` on ard's own PRs, pins the head, runs the agent on the diff, closes out through the shared Finisher (one PR review)\ninternal/triage/    the triage Loop: intake sweep, admission gate (body-hash re-gate), `after:` ordering, claim, watcher, fan-in gate (adds `ard:worker:<role>`); closes out through the shared Finisher\ninternal/labels/    the one place that spells an `ard:` label; catalogue from config\ninternal/labels/labelsync/  `ard labels sync`: diff catalogue vs repo, create/update via effects\ninternal/gitref/    ref-name checks shared by config and executor; leaf, stdlib only\ninternal/repopath/  owner/repo to lowercased, nested local path segments (state, lock, work, logs); leaf, stdlib only\ninternal/initcmd/   ard init: proposes the starter .ard/ as a PR on ard-init\ninternal/roles/     starter .ard/ tree (config.yaml, role dirs) embedded for ard init\ninternal/           config, github, committer, executor, agent, scanner, state, ...\nDockerfile          the ard image (ghcr.io/albertcbrown/ard): ard plus the cve tools; no RUN on the target platform\ndeploy/argocd/      ard-root app-of-apps chart (one Application per target repo); deploy/charts/ard-serve/ one ard serve\ndeploy/test-*.sh    chart, image and repo checks for the above (`make chart-test`, `make image-test`)\ndocs/proposal.md    design source of truth\n```\n\nNew subcommands: implement the body in its own package as\n`func(ctx context.Context, e *cli.Env) error`, then add one field to\n`cli.Handlers` and one wiring line in `cmd/ard/main.go`'s `handlers()`.\nA nil handler runs the command as a \"not implemented yet\" stub.\n\n## Commands\n\n```bash\nmake build   # bin/ard\nmake test    # go test ./...\nmake lint    # go vet + gofmt check\nmake fmt     # gofmt -w\n```\n\nRun `make lint test` before every commit.\n\n## Conventions\n\n- Tests: table-driven, no network. Use the GitHub fake for unit tests; real-API tests go behind the `integration` build tag.\n- Errors: wrap with context (`fmt.Errorf(\"claim issue %d: %w\", n, err)`); never swallow.\n- Logging: structured (`log/slog`), one event per claim, close-out and failure.\n- Keep packages small and interfaces at the consumer.\n\n## Workflow\n\n- Work is tracked in GitHub issues with `phase:N`, `area:*` and `type:*` labels and a phase milestone. Pick from the lowest open phase.\n- Branch per issue: `<issue#>-<short-slug>`. Open a PR that says `Closes #<issue>`. Do not push to `main` directly.\n- Keep PRs to one issue. Note follow-ups as new issues rather than widening the PR.\n- Decision issues (`type:decision`) record the choice in a comment, then update the proposal and close.\n- No AI attribution in commits or PRs: no `Co-Authored-By: Claude …` trailers, and no \"Generated with Claude Code\" lines in PR descriptions or comments.\nVERSION ?= $(shell git describe --tags --always --dirty 2>/dev/null || echo dev)\nLDFLAGS := -s -w -X main.version=$(VERSION)\n\n.PHONY: build test lint fmt clean cve-fixtures chart-test image-test\n\nbuild:\n\tCGO_ENABLED=0 go build -ldflags \"$(LDFLAGS)\" -o bin/ard ./cmd/ard\n\ntest:\n\tgo test ./...\n\nlint:\n\tgo vet ./...\n\t@out=\"$$(gofmt -l .)\"; if [ -n \"$$out\" ]; then echo \"gofmt needed:\"; echo \"$$out\"; exit 1; fi\n\nfmt:\n\tgofmt -w .\n\n# Renders the two Argo CD charts under deploy/ and checks them (needs helm and yq;\n# run by the ard-ci pipeline, not part of `make test`).\nchart-test:\n\tdeploy/test-charts.sh\n\n# Builds the ard image for both platforms and checks it (needs Docker buildx and\n# network; not part of `make test`).\nimage-test:\n\tdeploy/test-image.sh\n\n# Regenerates the real cve scanner fixtures inside the pinned tool image.\n# Needs Docker and network; not part of `make test`.\ncve-fixtures:\n\tdocker build -t ard-cve-fixtures internal/roles/scanners/cve\n\tdocker run --rm --user \"$$(id -u):$$(id -g)\" -e HOME=/tmp \\\n\t  -v \"$$PWD/internal/roles:/roles\" -w /roles ard-cve-fixtures sh testdata/cve/regen.sh\n\nclean:\n\trm -rf bin\n.ard/reviewers:\neng-review\n\ninternal:\nagent\nagentjob\nbotcheck\ncli\ncloseout\ncommitter\nconfig\nconfigcmd\nconfigsrc\ncontroller\ncostblock\ncosts\neffects\nexecutor\ngithub\ngitref\ngitx\ninitcmd\nintake\nlabels\nlimits\nmodelauth\npool\nreaper\nredact\nrepopath\nreviewer\nroles\nscanner\nservecmd\nstate\ntriage\nworker\nwtlock\n\ninternal/agent:\nagent.go\nagent_test.go\nclaude\nerrors.go\n\ninternal/agentjob:\nadmit.go\nadmit_test.go\ncommand.go\ndoc.go\njob.go\njob_test.go\nsnapshot.go\nsnapshot_test.go\ntestdata\n\ninternal/botcheck:\nbotcheck_test.go\ndoc.go\n\ninternal/cli:\napp.go\ncli_test.go\ncmd_config.go\ncmd_init.go\ncmd_labels.go\ncmd_rebuild.go\ncmd_serve.go\ncmd_version.go\nlog.go\nlog_test.go\nroot.go\nrun.go\n\ninternal/closeout:\nallow.go\nallow_test.go\ncause_test.go\ncloseout.go\ncloser_test.go\ncomment.go\ncomment_test.go\ncommit.go\ndoc.go\nenv_test.go\nfacts.go\nleadworker_test.go\nnext.go\npaths.go\npaths_test.go\nplan.go\nplan_merge.go\nplan_merge_test.go\nplan_test.go\nprepare.go\nprepare_allow_test.go\nprepare_test.go\nprgone_test.go\nreview.go\nreview_test.go\nrolefile.go\nrolefile_test.go\ntestdata\nusage.go\nusage_test.go\nworker.go\nworker_test.go\n\ninternal/committer:\nbranches.go\ncode.go\ncode_test.go\ncodepath.go\ncodepath_test.go\ncommitter.go\ncommitter_test.go\nerrors.go\nguard.go\ninit.go\ninit_test.go\nmessage.go\nrefs.go\nrefs_test.go\nrequest.go\nunit_test.go\n\ninternal/config:\nafter.go\nafter_test.go\ndefaults.go\ndiscover.go\nduration.go\nduration_test.go\nerrors.go\nload.go\nload_test.go\nprotected.go\nprotected_test.go\nraw.go\nrender.go\nrender_test.go\nroot.go\nsource.go\nsource_test.go\ntestdata\ntypes.go\n\ninternal/configcmd:\nconfig.go\nconfig_test.go\n\ninternal/configsrc:\nconfigsrc.go\nconfigsrc_test.go\n\ninternal/controller:\ncontroller.go\ndoc.go\nhelpers_test.go\ninflight.go\nlock_other.go\nlock_unix.go\nlock_unix_test.go\nloop.go\npause.go\npause_test.go\npausefile_test.go\nscheduler.go\nscheduler_test.go\nshutdown_test.go\nstatus.go\nstatus_test.go\n\ninternal/costblock:\ncostblock.go\ncostblock_test.go\nlocks.go\n\ninternal/costs:\ncosts.go\ncosts_test.go\nrows.go\n\ninternal/effects:\narchtest\ndryrun.go\neffects.go\neffects_test.go\nledger.go\nlive.go\nopen.go\n\ninternal/executor:\nerrors.go\nexecutor.go\nlocal\nvalidate.go\nvalidate_test.go\n\ninternal/github:\nauth.go\nauth_test.go\nclient.go\nclienttest\nconvert.go\ndoc.go\nerrors.go\nfake\ngraphql.go\nhttp.go\nhttp_test.go\nintegration_test.go\nquery\nrepo.go\nrepo_test.go\ntestdata\ntransport.go\ntransport_test.go\ntypes.go\n\ninternal/gitref:\ngitref.go\ngitref_test.go\n\ninternal/gitx:\nexport_test.go\ngittest\ngitx.go\ngitx_test.go\ninspect.go\ninspect_test.go\nlocal.go\nreview.go\nreview_test.go\nshallow_test.go\nsnapshot.go\nversion.go\nversion_test.go\nwrite.go\n\ninternal/initcmd:\nbody.go\ninitcmd.go\ninitcmd_test.go\nplan.go\n\ninternal/intake:\nintake.go\nintake_test.go\n\ninternal/labels:\ncatalogue.go\ncatalogue_test.go\ncolors.go\nlabels.go\nlabels_test.go\nlabelsync\nliteral_test.go\ntestdata\n\ninternal/limits:\nlimits.go\nlimits_test.go\n\ninternal/modelauth:\nmodelauth.go\nmodelauth_test.go\nsmoke_integration_test.go\n\ninternal/pool:\ncandidate.go\nclass.go\ndemand.go\npool.go\npool_test.go\nslot.go\nstats.go\n\ninternal/reaper:\ndecide.go\ndecide_test.go\ndoc.go\nfinish.go\nfinishwith_test.go\nharness_test.go\nlimit.go\nlimit_test.go\nnorecord.go\nreaper.go\nreaper_test.go\nretention.go\nusage_test.go\n\ninternal/redact:\ndoc.go\nentropy.go\ngitleaks.go\ngitleaks_integration_test.go\ngitleaks_test.go\nhelpers_test.go\nhostpath.go\nhostpath_test.go\nmulti.go\npath.go\npath_test.go\nrace_off_test.go\nrace_on_test.go\nredact.go\nredact_test.go\nreplace.go\nrules.go\nscan.go\nscan_test.go\nsecrets.go\nsecrets_test.go\ntruncate.go\ntruncate_test.go\n\ninternal/repopath:\nrepopath.go\nrepopath_test.go\n\ninternal/reviewer:\nbranch.go\ncandidates.go\ncandidates_test.go\ndoc.go\nharness_test.go\ninput.go\nloop.go\nready.go\nready_test.go\nrun.go\nrun_test.go\nunit_test.go\n\ninternal/roles:\nconfig.yaml\ncve_docker_integration_test.go\ncve_integration_test.go\ncve_test.go\neng_review_test.go\nengineer_test.go\nlead_test.go\nreviewers\nroles.go\nroles_test.go\nscanners\ntestdata\ntriage\nworker_engineer_test.go\nworkers\n\ninternal/scanner:\ndecode.go\ndiff.go\ndoc.go\nengine_exec.go\nengine_exec_test.go\nfinding.go\nfinding_test.go\ngroup.go\nharness_test.go\nloop.go\nloop_test.go\nmarker.go\nmarker_test.go\npipeline.go\npipeline_test.go\nplan_test.go\nrebuild.go\nrebuild_test.go\nreconcile.go\nsnapshot.go\nstale\nstore.go\ntestdata\n\ninternal/servecmd:\nadopt.go\nadopt_test.go\ncfgloader.go\ncfgloader_test.go\nclone.go\nclone_test.go\ncommitters.go\nlimits_test.go\nloops_test.go\nreaping.go\nreaping_test.go\nrebuild.go\nrebuild_test.go\nreviewer.go\nreviewer_e2e_test.go\nscanners.go\nscanners_test.go\nserve.go\nserve_test.go\ntriage.go\ntriage_e2e_test.go\ntriage_test.go\nwake_other.go\nwake_unix.go\nwake_unix_test.go\nworker.go\nworker_e2e_test.go\n\ninternal/state:\nadmissions.go\nadmissions_test.go\ncosttables.go\ncosttables_test.go\ndir.go\ndir_test.go\nevents.go\ngolden_test.go\njobs.go\nmigrations\nreaper_test.go\nscannerdb\nsqlitex\nstore.go\nstore_test.go\ntestdata\nusage.go\nusage_test.go\n\ninternal/triage:\ncandidates.go\ndoc.go\nfanin.go\nfanin_test.go\ngate_test.go\nharness_test.go\nintake.go\nintake_test.go\nloop.go\nloop_test.go\norder.go\norder_test.go\nrun.go\nsnapshot_test.go\n\ninternal/worker:\nbranch.go\ncandidates.go\ncandidates_test.go\ndoc.go\nfacts.go\nguard.go\nharness_test.go\nloop.go\nloop_test.go\npr.go\nrun.go\ntype.go\nunit_test.go\n\ninternal/wtlock:\nwtlock.go\nwtlock_test.go\n(eval):1: no matches found: --include=*.go\n258:Local-executor details: the role directory (and its `Dockerfile`) is mounted from the config snapshot at the job's SHA, not from the working tree. The job worktree is always detached at a SHA resolved from the job's ref (the executor never checks out a branch; the committer owns branch worktrees). The controller's clone is a bare repository ard owns at `~/.ard/repos/<owner>/<repo>.git` (lowercased), with no remote, no stored credential and auto-gc off (`gc.auto=0`, `maintenance.auto=false`). `ard serve` creates it on first start and seeds it with one fetch of `main` (up to 30 min; if that fails, serve starts paused and the next start retries), and later starts reuse it. Config loads and committer lanes fetch into it, and every committer and executor worktree is cut from it (lanes under `~/.ard/work/<owner>/<repo>/lanes/`). `ard serve` never writes to the operator's checkout: it reads it only for the `origin` fallback of `--repo` and the local `paused` key. To rebuild a damaged clone, stop serve, `rm -rf` it and restart. Upgrading from a version that used the checkout: ard deletes its old lane and job worktree directories without running git there, so the old `refs/ard/*` refs and the dangling worktree registrations stay in the checkout until you remove them (`git for-each-ref --format='delete %(refname)' refs/ard/ | git update-ref --stdin; git worktree prune`). Every `git worktree add`, `remove` and `prune` on the controller's clone, by the executor and the committer's lanes alike, takes one in-process lock keyed by the clone's common git directory, because git's worktree bookkeeping is not safe under concurrent admin commands; fetches, commits and pushes do not take it. Each such command also has a 30 s deadline, and cleanup runs even when the job's context is cancelled, so one hung git cannot stall every lane. Do not run `git worktree prune` (or add/remove) in `~/.ard/repos/<owner>/<repo>.git` by hand while `ard serve` runs: the lock is per process and cannot cover a human's git. The operator's own checkout is free to use. The host keeps two files per job: `<base>.log` (stdout and stderr combined, the heartbeat source and the committed log) and `<base>.stdout` (stdout only, parsed by the adapter). A read-only job (`ReadOnly`, used by scanners) gets the worktree mounted `:ro` under Docker, or `chmod -R a-w` on the host. The heartbeat is the log file's mtime while the PID is alive; liveness alone is not a beat. Phase 0 local jobs do not survive a controller restart: the pipes belong to the controller, so after a restart the job reports `orphaned` (PID alive, start time verified) or `gone`, and the Reaper kills and fails it. The executor is not an effect: it never calls GitHub or commits, so jobs run normally under `--dry-run`. Sidecar auth: the sidecar fetch runs in the controller with the GitHub App installation token in a host-scoped `http.<url>.extraheader` passed through git's environment, never argv; the job receives plain files and no credential, and the cache stores no remote and no token. The App must be installed on the sidecar repo with contents:read. Host-mode jobs can reach `~/.ard/sidecars`, but it holds no credential. Host-mode jobs run as the controller's uid: they can read the GitHub App key and `~/.config/gh` by absolute path, write the controller's clone, and undo `chmod a-w`. Host mode is allowed for every role ([#84](https://github.com/albertcbrown/ard/issues/84)), but a role that reads untrusted input (every triage, worker and reviewer role, and an LLM scanner) warns loudly when it has no `Dockerfile`; deterministic scanners are exempt, and nothing warns under `executor: k8s`, where every job runs in a container. `ard config check` and the first successful config load of `ard serve` (and any role a later config adds) log one `executor.host_untrusted` WARN per such role and also write it to stderr, so it survives `--log-level`. The local executor logs the WARN again for every host-mode job of such a role and appends a `host_untrusted` row to the `events` table. Each warning lists what the job can reach, including `~/.ard` (state, config snapshots, the pause file, other jobs' worktrees and logs). The owner accepts this risk while ard runs only on owner-controlled repos, and revisits it when moving to the spark cluster, where every job runs in a container once the k8s executor (#54) lands. Until then the Gate 1 dry-run soak on spark (see Rollout and Delivery) runs `ard serve` with the local executor in a pod, and the deterministic cve scanner runs there as a host subprocess of the controller container: its role directory has no `Dockerfile`, and the `ard` image carries its pinned tools. Only deterministic scanners run in that pod: it starts `ard serve --loops scanners,stale,reaper` ([#187](https://github.com/albertcbrown/ard/issues/187)), so the triage, worker and reviewer loops never run there, even though `.ard/config.yaml` on `main` has agent roles. `--loops` takes a comma-separated list of loop names (scanners, stale, triage, workers, reviewers, reaper), defaults to every loop, and rejects an unknown name with exit 2. `ard serve` and `ard config check` refuse to run on git older than 2.47.0, because the committer's fetches fail on older versions.\n259-\n260-A worker job is several executor jobs (`<id>`, `<id>t1`, `<id>f1`, ...) under one state job and one slot; the Reaper watches whichever step is stored on the job, so its heartbeat and deadline rules apply to tests too.\n261-\n262-**One pool of job slots, drained from the end.** At most `max_pods` jobs (default 3) run at once, shared by every loop. When a slot frees, the controller fills it in this order: reviewers, then workers, then triage, then scanners, oldest first within each. Finishing work in flight beats starting new work, so a busy day grows the scanner backlog, not the pile of half-done PRs. The merger and the Reaper are controller code and take no slot. Slots are granted when a loop polls and nothing is queued: a loop that found work waiting within the last two of its intervals has that demand reserved ahead of lower classes, so a freed slot can sit idle until that loop's next poll. A scanner run that gets no slot is skipped, not queued. The pool also starts no agent job for an exhausted account or model family until its reset: a job that stops at a subscription usage limit records the limit (in the controller database, so a restart keeps it), and the pool answers `limited` to any candidate whose role runs on that account or family (a whole-account session or weekly limit blocks every role; an Opus or Sonnet limit blocks only roles on that family, and a role with no model, which runs the CLI default, is blocked by any limit). A limited candidate takes no slot and reserves no demand, so lower classes are not held back. The block ends at the reset time the CLI reported; when it reported none the first pause is 30 minutes and each further stop with no reset doubles it up to 4 hours, starting over once a job succeeds; a reset time further away than 8 days is clamped. Running jobs are never killed for a limit. Deterministic scanners and the merger use no model and are unaffected.\n263-\n264-|  | Local executor | Kubernetes executor |\n265-| --- | --- | --- |\n266-| Workspace | `git worktree` of the controller's clone (`~/.ard/repos/<owner>/<repo>.git`) under `~/.ard/work/<owner>/<repo>/<job-id>`; sidecar exported into the job home | `Job` with an init container that shallow-clones the branch into an `emptyDir`; sidecar cloned by an init container (Phase 3) |\n267-| Runtime | the role's `Dockerfile` image under Docker if it has one, else a host subprocess; hard timeout | a pod from the role's `Dockerfile` image, or the default `ard-agent` image; hard timeout |\n268-| Heartbeat | PID alive plus log file mtime | pod phase plus container restarts |\n269-| Logs | streamed to `~/.ard/logs/<owner>/<repo>/<issue#>/` while running; committed to `.ard/<issue#>/logs/` at close-out | pod logs, committed to `.ard/<issue#>/logs/` at close-out |\n270-| Labels on resources | job id in the directory name | `ard/issue`, `ard/stage`, `ard/role`, `ard/job` |\n271-| Good for | Phase 1, one host, fast iteration | parallelism, isolation, untrusted inputs |\n272-\n273-**Agent CLIs plug in through adapters.** Each adapter turns (prompt file, context paths, budget) into a command line and parses the exit code and, where the CLI reports them, turns and cost. For Claude Code that is `claude -p --output-format=stream-json --verbose --restricted --safe-mode --strict-mcp-config --disable-slash-commands --permission-mode=dontAsk --settings='{\"includeGitInstructions\":false,\"disableAllHooks\":true}' --tools=… --allowed-tools=… --disallowed-tools=… --model <model> --max-budget-usd <max_budget_usd> --append-system-prompt-file <role prompt> -- <message>` (`--verbose` is required by `stream-json` in print mode). Every stage (scanner, triage, worker, review) gets the same tool profile: `--tools` and `--allowed-tools` are `Read,Glob,Grep,Edit,Write,Bash`, and `--disallowed-tools` is `Bash(git commit *),Bash(git push *),Bash(git remote *),Bash(gh *)` (decided, #79). ard does not pass `--bare`: it limits the CLI to Bash, Read and Edit, and `--tools` cannot widen it (#105). The planted-config isolation `--bare` gave comes instead from `--restricted` (only managed settings and `--settings` load), `--safe-mode` (no CLAUDE.md or AGENTS.md, skills, agents, hooks, MCP servers or auto memory), `--strict-mcp-config`, `--disable-slash-commands`, and `--settings` that turns off hooks and the git status snapshot (commit subjects are third-party text). `CLAUDE_CODE_DISABLE_BACKGROUND_TASKS=1` is set as a fixed env var (the executor allows it) so Bash cannot detach commands. The integration tests pin each of these against a worktree with planted config and must be re-run on every `TestedVersion` bump. The tool list is not a security boundary, and the deny list is advisory, because shell indirection gets around it. The real boundaries are the missing GitHub token (Docker; see the host-mode note above, #84), the committer, which commits only `.ard/<issue#>/` from a triage or review job (other edits are discarded with the worktree), and the diff guard on worker changes. An explicit tool list from the caller (`Spec.Tools`) replaces the allow list and keeps the deny list. The user message holds only fixed text and the paths of the untrusted input files. The installed CLI accepts an undocumented `--max-turns`; ard does not rely on it, so a per-job dollar cap and the executor's hard timeout bound each run. Agents run with an empty per-job CLI config dir and HOME and receive exactly one model credential, passed by name by the executor and never visible to the adapter: an API key (`ANTHROPIC_API_KEY`) or a subscription OAuth token (`CLAUDE_CODE_OAUTH_TOKEN`, from `claude setup-token`); that rests on the environment, not on a CLI flag. `ard serve` chooses the credential from its own environment: `ARD_AGENT_AUTH` is `auto` (the default; the token if set, else the key), `oauth` or `apikey`, and an explicit mode never falls back to the other variable. Only one is ever passed, because the CLI prefers the API key and would silently bill it (pinned by an integration test). A missing credential does not stop startup, because deterministic scanners need none: `ard serve` logs one INFO line, and a job that needs one fails with a clear error. The executor creates the per-job config dir as `<job home>/.claude` (0700) in `prepare` (`Handle.View.ConfigDir`; under Docker it is a bind mount at `/ard-config`), refuses to start a job whose config dir is not that empty directory, and removes it with the job home; the Reaper's `KeepWorktree` retention keeps it too, and it holds no credential, since the credenti","stderr":"","interrupted":false,"isImage":false,"noOutputExpected":false,"persistedOutputPath":"/ard-home/.claude/projects/-Users-al--ard-work-albertcbrown-ard-01M84F29DFC744WW354H9W6R8Y/d5030d13-ac14-409f-95ab-4d8af437021d/tool-results/bpmpgipt4.txt","persistedOutputSize":47794},"tool_result_meta":[{"id":"toolu_01BRa1D8E7fUPm1oxkvuGkuR","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"rule"}}]}
